# Compiler Design Course Project — Project Proposal

Submitted by
Vichruth

B.Tech Computer Science, VIT Vellore

Project Overview
This project is a compiler for standard textbook pseudocode (IF/THEN/ELSE, FOR, WHILE, REPEAT/UNTIL, FUNCTIONs, arrays), built from scratch in C. Rather than stopping at a single interpreter, the compiler is designed around a dual-backend architecture: a shared intermediate representation (IR) that can be executed two different ways depending on the use case.

What Makes This More Than a Toy Compiler
Dual-backend execution: one frontend (lexer, parser, semantic analysis) feeds a common IR, which is then consumed by two separate backends — a bytecode compiler + stack-based VM (fast, good for iteration and debugging), and an ahead-of-time (AOT) code generator that emits standalone C source, compiled by gcc into a native binary with zero runtime dependency. This mirrors how production compiler infrastructure like LLVM is structured — one IR, multiple backends.
Edge-deployment angle: the AOT-to-C backend opens the door to running compiled pseudocode on resource-constrained hardware (e.g. microcontrollers), tying the project to real deployment scenarios rather than staying purely academic.
Compiler Pipeline
Lexer — hand-written tokenizer for the pseudocode grammar
Parser — recursive descent parser producing an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST)
Semantic Analysis — scope resolution and type checking
Codegen — AST lowered into a common IR, then compiled to bytecode
Execution — bytecode VM (stack-based) or AOT compilation to C via gcc
